---
import dedent from "dedent";

import RevealLayout from "../../layouts/PresentationLayout.astro";
import {
  RubikCube as RubikCubeViz,
  EncodingShowcase,
  CubeCompare,
  StickerPermutation,
} from "../../components/cave/rubik-cube";
import { RubikStateGraph } from "../../components/cave/rubik-graph";
import { LogoAnimation } from "../../components/cave/intro";
import PyodideWorkerRunner from "../../../src/components/PyodideWorkerRunner";
import RevealCode from "../../components/RevealCode";

export const stickerPermutationCode = dedent`\
    MOVES = {
        "R": [0, 9, 2, 11, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 21, 10, 23, 14, 12, 15, 13, 3, 17, 1, 19, 20, 18, 22, 16],
        # other moves...
    }

    def apply_move(state, move):
        return "".join(state[i] for i in move)

    start = "BBBBRRRRYYYYOOOOWWWWGGGG"
    print(apply_move(start, MOVES["R"]))`;

export const naiveCode = dedent`\
    MOVES = {
        "R": [0, 9, 2, 11, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 21, 10, 23, 14, 12, 15, 13, 3, 17, 1, 19, 20, 18, 22, 16],
        "D": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 18, 19, 8, 9, 6, 7, 12, 13, 10, 11, 16, 17, 14, 15, 22, 20, 23, 21],
        "B": [13, 15, 2, 3, 1, 5, 0, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 23, 14, 22, 18, 16, 19, 17, 20, 21, 4, 6],
        "F": [0, 1, 7, 5, 4, 20, 6, 21, 10, 8, 11, 9, 2, 13, 3, 15, 16, 17, 18, 19, 14, 12, 22, 23],
        "U": [2, 0, 3, 1, 8, 9, 6, 7, 12, 13, 10, 11, 16, 17, 14, 15, 4, 5, 18, 19, 20, 21, 22, 23],
        "L": [19, 1, 17, 3, 6, 4, 7, 5, 0, 9, 2, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 22, 18, 20, 8, 21, 10, 23],
    }


    def invert_permutation(perm):
        inv = [0] * len(perm)
        for i, p in enumerate(perm):
            inv[p] = i
        return inv


    def compose(p2, p1):
        return [p1[i] for i in p2]


    def get_moves(inverse=True, half_turn=True):
        moves = {}
        for k, v in MOVES.items():
            moves[k] = v
            if inverse:
                moves[k + "'"] = invert_permutation(v)
            if half_turn:
                moves[k + "2"] = compose(v, v)
        return moves


    def apply_move(state, move):
        return "".join(state[i] for i in move)


    def bfs(start, moves, max_depth):
        visited = {start: 0}
        frontier = {start}

        for depth in range(1, max_depth + 1):
            print("Starting Depth", depth)
            next_frontier = set()

            for state in frontier:
                for move in moves.values():
                    nxt = apply_move(state, move)
                    if nxt not in visited:
                        visited[nxt] = depth
                        next_frontier.add(nxt)

            frontier = next_frontier
            print("Finished Depth", depth)
            print("    next frontier size", len(frontier))
            print("    visited so far", len(visited))

        return visited


    def main():
        initial_state = "BBBBRRRRYYYYOOOOWWWWGGGG"
        moves = get_moves(inverse=True, half_turn=True)
        visited = bfs(initial_state, moves, 20)
        counts = {}
        for dist in visited.values():
            counts[dist] = counts.get(dist, 0) + 1
        print("Total states visited:", len(visited))
        print("Move counts by distance:", counts)


    main()`;
---

<RevealLayout
  title="The 2x2x2 Rubik's Cube"
  description="Efficient representations and traversal of the Rubik's Cube state space"
  theme="dark"
>
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        The Rubik's Cube has an absurd number of possible configurations. Far
        too many to explore, even with a modern computer. But what about its
        little brother, the 2 by 2? Is its universe small enough to explore
        completely?
      </p>
      <p>
        And not just explore it. Can we compute the minimum number of moves
        needed to solve every single cube? Spoiler: yes, we can. And we'll do it
        in Python, directly in the browser. But getting there is not trivial.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <CubeCompare client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <section id="intro">
    <LogoAnimation
      client:load
      width={2080}
      height={600}
      pixelScale={8}
      duration={2700}
      loop={true}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Before doing any math or writing any code, we need to be precise about
        what we are measuring. What does it mean to say that a cube is, for
        example, 7 moves away from solved? Well, that depends on what counts as
        a move.
      </p>
      <p>
        In the cubing community, each face is named relative to the current
        orientation of the cube. So the right face does not mean a fixed color.
        It simply means whatever face is on the right from our point of view.
      </p>
      <p>
        For example, the orange face might currently be on the right. But if we
        rotate the whole cube, that same orange face might become the front face
        instead.
      </p>
      <p>
        Using this notation, an R move means a clockwise turn of the right face.
        Its inverse, R prime, means turning that same face counter-clockwise.
        And we could also decide that a 180 degree turn counts as a basic move.
        In that case, we call it R2. So the minimum number of moves depends on
        which moves we take as basic.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <RubikCubeViz client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <section id="graph">
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Once we know which moves are allowed, the problem becomes much easier to
        think about. We can represent all cube configurations as a graph. Each
        configuration is a node. And there is an edge between two nodes if one
        allowed move transforms one state into the other.
      </p>
      <p>
        To make this concrete, imagine that we only allow one move: R. Starting
        from the solved cube, applying R once gives us a new state. Applying it
        again gives another. Two more turns bring us back to solved.
      </p>
      <p>
        So if we only allow R, the entire reachable universe has just four
        states.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now notice something important. If we only allow R, one of those states
        is 3 moves away from solved. But if we also allow R prime, that same
        state is only 1 move away. So the minimum number of moves depends on
        which moves we allow.
      </p>
      <p>
        If only quarter turns count as single moves, this is called the
        quarter-turn metric. If half turns such as R2 also count as single
        moves, this is called the half-turn metric.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is no need to define R3, because that is just the same as R prime.
        So for each face, we really have three meaningful moves: clockwise,
        counter-clockwise, and half turn.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <RubikStateGraph
      client:only="react"
      initialIndex={0}
      initialDepth={4}
      initialMoves={["R", "R'", "R2"]}
      overflow={true}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Now the problem is well defined. We have a graph. We have a move set.
        But a computer still needs one more thing. It needs a way to represent
        each cube state. In other words, it needs an encoding.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <EncodingShowcase client:only="react" width={1080} height={600} />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        A very natural first approach is to represent the cube using its
        stickers. We choose a fixed order for the 24 visible stickers and store
        the state as a string. Each character tells us the color of one sticker.
        So every cube state becomes just a different string. That gives us
        something a computer can actually manipulate.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <RubikCubeViz
      client:only="react"
      showStateEditor={true}
      cameraRelativeEncoding={true}
    />
  </section>

  <section id="sticker-perm">
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Once the cube is encoded as a string, the next question is how to apply
        moves. Each move simply rearranges some of the stickers. For example, in
        the solved cube, an R move sends this yellow sticker from the front face
        to the up face. In our encoding, that sticker is at index 9, so after an
        R move it ends up at index 1.
      </p>
      <p>
        Of course, a move doesn't affect just one sticker. It moves several of
        them at the same time. And, it doesn't matter what the current cube
        looks like. The move always rearranges the same positions in the same
        way.
      </p>
      <p>
        So in the end, every move is just a fixed reordering of sticker
        positions. In mathematics, that is called a permutation.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <StickerPermutation client:only="react" visibleStickers={24} />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        So in code, each move can be stored as a list of indices. For example,
        if the second value in the list is 9, that means the sticker that used
        to be at index 9 now moves into position 1. Then applying a move is just
        building a new string by reading the old one in that new order.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <RevealCode
      client:only="react"
      animation="fade"
      code={`MOVES = {
    "R": [0, 9, 2, 11, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 21, 10, 23, 14, 12, 15, 13, 3, 17, 1, 19, 20, 18, 22, 16],
    # other moves...
}

# STEP-1
def apply_move(state, move):
    return "".join(state[i] for i in move)

# STEP-2
start_state = "BBBBRRRRYYYYOOOOWWWWGGGG"
next_state = apply_move(start_state, MOVES["R"])
print(next_state)  # BYBYRRRRYGYGOOOOBWBWGWGW`}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Now that we can represent a state and apply moves, we can start
        exploring the state space. We begin from the solved cube. Then we apply
        all possible moves to find every state that is 1 move away. Then we
        apply all moves again to those states to find every state that is 2
        moves away. Then 3 moves away. And so on.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is breadth-first search, or BFS. And BFS is exactly what we want,
        because it explores states in order of distance from the start. So the
        first time we reach a state, we know we have found a shortest path to
        it.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can picture BFS as a wave expanding outward from the solved state.
        At each step, the wave reaches one new layer. That layer is often called
        the frontier.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <RubikStateGraph
      client:only="react"
      initialIndex={0}
      initialDepth={3}
      initialMoves={["F", "R"]}
      overflow={true}
    />
  </section>

  <section id="wip">
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Let's implement BFS in Python using frontiers. We keep a dictionary
        called visited. It tells us which states we have already seen, and how
        far they are from solved
      </p>
      <p>
        We start with a frontier containing only the solved state. Then we build
        the next frontier. For each state in the current frontier, we apply all
        possible moves. If the new state has not been seen before, we store it
        in visited and add it to the next frontier. Then we repeat the process
        with that new frontier. And this works. At least in theory.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <RevealCode
      client:only="react"
      animation="fade"
      code={`def bfs(start, max_depth):
    # STEP-1
    visited = {start: 0}
    frontier = {start}

    # STEP-2
    for depth in range(1, max_depth + 1):
        next_frontier = set()

        # STEP-3
        for state in frontier:
            for move in MOVES:
                nxt = apply_move(state, move)
                # STEP-4
                if nxt not in visited:
                    visited[nxt] = depth
                    next_frontier.add(nxt)

        # STEP-5
        frontier = next_frontier

    # STEP-6
    return visited`}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        When we run the code in Pyodide, a Python interpreter for the browser,
        we hit its memory limit of around 2 GB.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <PyodideWorkerRunner client:only="react" initialCode={naiveCode} />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        If we run the same code outside the browser, using a regular Python
        interpreter, it does finish. But it takes around 34 minutes and peaks at
        more than 13 GB of memory. The next step is not to change the algorithm,
        but to examine the representation more carefully.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <div class="flex justify-center">
      <div
        class="grid grid-cols-[max-content_max-content] gap-x-4 gap-y-5 text-2xl"
      >
        <span class="text-right">Total execution time:</span>
        <span class="font-bold text-3xl">34 min 50 s</span>
        <span class="text-right">Peak memory consumption:</span>
        <span class="font-bold text-3xl">13.15 GB</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        This is the number of states we have at each distance from solved. Looks
        like it found a total of 88 M states.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <div class="mt-10 flex justify-center">
      <div
        class="grid grid-cols-[max-content_max-content_1fr] items-center gap-x-4 gap-y-2 text-lg font-mono"
      >
        <span class="text-right text-gray-400">depth</span>
        <span class="text-right text-gray-400">states</span>
        <span></span>
        {
          [
            [0, 1],
            [1, 18],
            [2, 243],
            [3, 2874],
            [4, 28000],
            [5, 205416],
            [6, 1168516],
            [7, 5402628],
            [8, 20776176],
            [9, 45391616],
            [10, 15139616],
            [11, 64736],
          ].map(([d, n]) => (
            <>
              <span class="text-right text-gray-400">{d}</span>
              <span class="text-right tabular-nums">
                {n.toLocaleString("en-US")}
              </span>
              <div class="h-4 rounded-sm bg-amber-400/30">
                <div
                  class="h-full rounded-sm bg-amber-400/80"
                  style={`width: ${((n / 45391616) * 100).toFixed(2)}%`}
                />
              </div>
            </>
          ))
        }
        <span class="col-span-3 my-1 border-t border-gray-600"></span>
        <span class="text-right text-gray-400">total</span>
        <span class="text-right tabular-nums font-bold">88,179,840</span>
        <span></span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        There is an issue hiding inside our sticker encoding. We define our
        strings relative to the camera. So if we rotate the whole cube in space,
        the string changes. But from the puzzle's point of view, nothing
        meaningful has changed. It is the same physical cube state, just viewed
        from another angle. The computer sees those as different states. We do
        not. And this is the kind of trap that appears all the time in
        combinatorial problems. Symmetries can make the search space much larger
        than it really is. So we need a way to collapse all those rotated
        versions into one canonical representation.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <RubikCubeViz
      client:only="react"
      showStateEditor={true}
      cameraRelativeEncoding={true}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        A neat way to handle this is to choose one corner as a reference and
        always keep it in the same place. For example, we can always rotate the
        cube so that the blue-yellow-red corner ends up back in the front-left
        position. That does not change the puzzle state. It only changes how we
        look at it. And once we do that, all whole-cube rotations collapse into
        a single representation. The cube has 4 possible rotations for each
        choice of front face, and 6 possible front faces, so 24 in total. But
        there is more. Fixing one corner also makes some moves equivalent. For
        example, imagine starting from the solved cube, doing an L move, and
        then rotating the whole cube to put that reference corner back where it
        belongs. What you get is exactly the same normalized state you would get
        by starting from solved and doing an R move. So once we normalize the
        cube this way, some moves become redundant. That means we do not need
        all six face moves anymore. We only need the ones that leave the
        reference corner in place. For example: R, B, and D. That is great,
        because it removes branches from the search and avoids having to deal
        with whole-cube rotations separately.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <RubikCubeViz
      client:only="react"
      showStateEditor={true}
      cameraRelativeEncoding={false}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        With this change, we can solve all cube states in about 40 seconds using
        around 300 MB of memory, directly in Python and in the browser.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        The biggest improvement has not come from changing the algorithm. It has
        come from paying more attention to the representation. The algorithm is
        still breadth-first search. What changed is that we stopped treating the
        24 whole-cube rotations as different states. Once we do that, the
        problem becomes much more manageable. Can we keep improving the
        encoding? Of course. But that belongs in another video.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <PyodideWorkerRunner client:only="react" initialCode={naiveCode} />
  </section>
</RevealLayout>
